**Study:** [Engineering Dynamics — MIT 2.003SC, Fall 2011](../sources/Engineering-Dynamics-MIT-2-003SC.lingua#r_37MSK6EJHRV95GXWA23P4ZJPZ3)'s work–energy treatment and the shared energy/power Record. Follow the same boundary used for the force and momentum accounts.

A motor's power rating, a part's kinetic energy and the work done during a stroke are different quantities. To size an explanation, name the stroke, speed, load, losses and duration. A motor can transmit torque at zero angular velocity while its instantaneous mechanical output power is zero; holding a force does not necessarily mean no electrical consumption.

**Work:** Compare lifting the same mass through the same height slowly and quickly. Identify which ideal work stays equal, which peak power changes, and where acceleration, losses and energy recovery alter the real input. Keep the operating history for the later machine-element selection.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
